Design attribution
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

The idea this project builds on -- giving a coding agent an external,
dependency-aware issue graph so it keeps its bearings across sessions -- comes
from Beads ("bd"), by Steve Yegge and the Beads Contributors:

    https://github.com/steveyegge/beads
    Copyright (c) 2025 Beads Contributors
    MIT License

Several of this project's user-visible concepts were shaped by Beads: modelling
epics, tasks and subtasks as one issue kind distinguished by a type field;
expressing hierarchy as a parent-child edge in the dependency graph rather than
a column; the vocabulary of edge types (blocks, parent-child, related,
duplicates, supersedes); and the notion of "ready work" as the set of open
issues with no unfinished blockers.

This is an independent implementation, not a port. Beads is written in Go and
is backed by Dolt; this project is written in Python against SQLAlchemy with
SQLite or PostgreSQL, and exposes its functionality as MCP tools rather than a
CLI. The schema, service layer, query strategies, validation rules, error
types, and tests were designed and written for this codebase. No Beads source
code was copied.

Because no Beads code is included here, the MIT notice above is reproduced as
attribution rather than to satisfy a redistribution obligation. It is included
because the debt is real and worth stating plainly.

